WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:17.110
The Fastest, Most Direct Way to Learn a New
Language in 8 Lightning-quick Steps

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.110
1.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:19.660
Set language-learning goals.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.699
The first step to learning a new language
fast is to set goals for what you want to

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:24.699
achieve.

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:27.390
When you think about it, this makes a lot
of sense.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.130
If you don’t set goals, how can you know
what you want to achieve and measure whether

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:32.400
you have achieved it?

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.710
When faced with the idea of learning a new
language, most of us feel overwhelmed.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.530
There are so many words to learn and so many
different ways to study.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.720
Setting goals narrows your focus so you can
stop worrying about the details and get down

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.070
to business.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:51.280
Research shows that people who set the right
kind of goals are more likely to achieve success.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.370
Use these guidelines to get the most from
your goals:

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.890
Focus on specific, tangible outcomes.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.400
Set detailed goals, and focus on what you
plan to learn rather than how much time you

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.400
plan to study.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.909
An example of a good goal might be, “This
week I’m going to learn 30 Spanish vocabulary

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:10.920
words related to shopping.”

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.170
Set short-term goals.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.430
It’s good to have an ultimate goal—the
thing you eventually hope to achieve.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:21.659
But long-term goals are too overwhelming to
motivate you on an everyday basis.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:26.039
Break down your ultimate goal into smaller
bits, and set smaller goals for each week

00:01:26.039 --> 00:01:28.829
or month.

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:31.439
Challenge yourself (but not too much).

00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:33.799
Goals work best when they make you push yourself.

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:36.990
But if they’re too daunting, they can actually
discourage you.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.789
A good way to get around this is to set goals
with a range of outcomes.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:46.310
For example, you might say, “I want to learn
30-50 new vocabulary words this week.”

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.009
The lower number in this range helps you feel
the goal is achievable, while the higher number

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:53.789
allows you to push yourself.

00:01:53.789 --> 00:01:55.310
Write down your goals.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.320
Writing down goals helps you commit to them.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:02.150
Post your goals in a prominent place, like
your bathroom mirror or the home screen of

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.899
your smartphone.
2.

00:02:04.899 --> 00:02:08.069
Learn the “right” words.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:10.360
Languages are made up of a shocking number
of words.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:15.050
English, for example, has between 600,000
and 1 million words.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.220
Luckily, you don’t need to learn anywhere
near that many words to be proficient in a

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.220
language.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.940
Consider this: the top 100 words make up about
50 percent of English language texts, and

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.670
the top 1,000 words make up about 90 percent!

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.750
Check out these lists of the top 1,000 words
in these languages:

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:34.780
Spanish
French

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:35.810
German
Japanese

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.360
By focusing on learning these words first,
you can eliminate wasted time and increase

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.640
the amount of information you understand very
quickly.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.280
3.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.280
Study smart.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.810
When learning your words, you’ll learn faster
by using the very best study techniques.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:57.050
For example, one of the best ways to learn
vocabulary words is to use flashcards.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.100
Flashcards help you focus on individual words
and allow you to test yourself, which helps

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.950
you memorize new information.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:07.170
When you learn with flashcards, follow these
tips to learn fast:

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.660
Try out electronic flashcards.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.950
Paper flashcards work just as well as they
ever did, but electronic flashcard programs

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.480
like Anki provide some great benefits.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.510
By using electronic flashcards, you can easily
carry large stacks on your smartphone or tablet,

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:27.170
and you can take advantage of flashcards that
other people have created and made public.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:32.560
These programs also automatically change the
order of cards and use spaced repetition to

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.510
gradually increase the amount of time between
repetitions of a flashcard.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.560
Both of these techniques help you learn faster
and better.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.420
To maximize your use of SRS programs and electronic
flashcards, check out polyglot Olly Richards’

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.420
Conversations course, which is designed to
help you set up workable, step-by-step systems

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.420
for learning your target language.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:45.420
You can also get more targeted help with the
Uncovered courses, which introduces the basics

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.420
of specific languages including Spanish, French,
German and Italian.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.390
Make sure to guess the meaning of a word before
turning over the card.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.910
Flashcards work best when you use them to
test your memory, so don’t be too quick

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.530
to flip the cards over.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:57.580
Even if you don’t know a word, make a guess.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.890
Learn the translations first, then learn to
produce the new words.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.280
It’s easier to learn the translation of
a foreign word than it is to learn to say

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.750
the foreign word when you see its English
equivalent.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.870
Start by looking at the side of the flashcard
with a foreign word on it, and memorize what

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.460
the English translation is.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:19.849
Later, turn the cards over and use them to
practice producing the foreign words when

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:20.849
you see their English equivalents.

00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:21.849
Practice makes perfect, but effective practice
makes perfect even faster!

00:04:21.849 --> 00:04:22.849
Some more great strategies for integrating
new words alongside and beyond flashcards

00:04:22.849 --> 00:04:23.849
include:

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:24.849
Visualize and vocalize.

00:04:24.849 --> 00:04:25.849
Visualize the word you’re learning, imagine
the image of what it represents and say the

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:26.849
new word aloud.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:27.849
This helps you connect the concepts and can
improve memorization.

00:04:27.849 --> 00:04:28.849
Gesture.

00:04:28.849 --> 00:04:29.849
The brain learns better when you use physical
actions while learning.

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:30.849
Take advantage of this by gesturing.

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:31.849
If you want to learn the German word Schuh
(shoe), say the word while you pretend to

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:32.849
put on a shoe.

