00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:40.849
Use the word in your native language.

00:04:40.849 --> 00:04:41.849
When you’re learning a new language, it
can be hard to practice words in context because

00:04:41.849 --> 00:04:42.849
you haven’t yet mastered enough vocabulary
to make complex sentences.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:43.849
To get around this, simply use the word in
your native language.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:44.849
For example, if you’re learning the Spanish
word casa (house), you could say, “I’m

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:45.849
going to go to my casa now.”

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:46.849
Keyword technique.

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:47.849
Make up a sentence with the new word you’re
learning, the meaning of the word and a word

00:04:47.849 --> 00:04:48.849
in your native language that sounds similar.

00:04:48.849 --> 00:04:49.849
For example, if you want to learn the Spanish
word mesa (table), you could think of an English

00:04:49.849 --> 00:04:50.849
word that sounds similar and make up a sentence
like, “My kitchen table is always a mess!”

00:04:50.849 --> 00:04:51.849
Since “mess” and mesa are very similar,
this can help you remember the new word.

00:04:51.849 --> 00:04:52.849
4.

00:04:52.849 --> 00:04:53.849
Start using the language all day, every day.

00:04:53.849 --> 00:04:54.849
As a beginner, it can seem overwhelming to
try to use the language all day, but it’s

00:04:54.849 --> 00:04:55.849
not as difficult as it seems.

00:04:55.849 --> 00:04:56.849
There are many easy and even fun ways to make
the language a part of your regular life.

00:04:56.849 --> 00:04:57.849
First, make use of every moment you have to
learn new words.

00:04:57.849 --> 00:04:58.849
Take flashcards with you, and study them during
your train or bus commute (but not while driving,

00:04:58.849 --> 00:04:59.849
please!) or when you’re waiting to meet
a friend.

00:04:59.849 --> 00:05:00.849
When you start to feel tired, switch from
active learning to passive learning by doing

00:05:00.849 --> 00:05:01.849
what you would normally do in your native
language in your target language.

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:02.849
Try watching a video or TV show, or streaming
radio broadcasts in your target language.

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:03.849
There are many online resources to access
entertaining audio and video clips.

00:05:03.849 --> 00:05:04.849
You can go to YouTube, search for radio stations
available on the Internet or use FluentU.

00:05:04.849 --> 00:05:05.849
You may be asking, “How can I possibly watch
a video or listen to the radio when I only

00:05:05.849 --> 00:05:06.849
know a handful of words?”

00:05:06.849 --> 00:05:07.849
The goal when you start is not to understand
everything you hear but to familiarize yourself

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:08.849
with the sounds of the language.

00:05:08.849 --> 00:05:09.849
Even if you don’t understand much of what
you hear, simply listening can have many positive

00:05:09.849 --> 00:05:10.849
effects, including:

00:05:10.849 --> 00:05:11.849
Becoming accustomed to the cadence of the
language.

00:05:11.849 --> 00:05:12.849
Learning to identify and understand common
words.

00:05:12.849 --> 00:05:13.849
Learning to understand using only context
and a few cognates.

00:05:13.849 --> 00:05:14.849
Staying motivated!

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:15.849
5.

00:05:15.849 --> 00:05:16.849
Seek out real-life practice.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:17.849
Some of the best learning happens in real-life
situations, particularly when you have no

00:05:17.849 --> 00:05:18.849
choice but to use a foreign language.

00:05:18.849 --> 00:05:19.849
The easiest way to gain real-life practice
is to travel or study abroad.

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:20.849
Going abroad creates opportunities to be surrounded
by people who speak the language you want

00:05:20.849 --> 00:05:21.849
to learn, many of whom don’t know your native
language.

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:22.849
This is the favorite approach of organizations
like the Peace Corps, which regularly places

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:23.849
people with little or no knowledge of a language
into full immersion situations.

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:24.849
Although such situations can be uncomfortable,
they provide enormous motivation to learn

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:25.849
quickly.

00:05:25.849 --> 00:05:26.849
But even without traveling abroad, you can
immerse yourself in real-life situations that

00:05:26.849 --> 00:05:27.849
give you loads of language practice.

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:28.849
Try these options:

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:29.849
Meet with a language partner weekly or biweekly.

00:05:29.849 --> 00:05:30.849
You might pay your language partner for his
or her time or offer to exchange one hour

00:05:30.849 --> 00:05:31.849
of practice in the language you want to learn
for an hour of practice speaking English.

00:05:31.849 --> 00:05:32.849
Join a conversation club.

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:33.849
Many cities and schools have conversation
clubs where language students meet regularly

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:34.849
to practice having informal discussions in
their target language.

00:05:34.849 --> 00:05:35.849
Use an online tutoring or language partner
site.

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:36.849
Sites such as Coeffee.com or My Language Exchange
can introduce you to people who speak the

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:37.849
language you want to practice.

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:38.849
Even if you don’t see them in person, you
can gain real-life language practice by chatting

00:05:38.849 --> 00:05:39.849
online.

00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:40.849
Volunteer with immigrants in your city.

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:41.849
Find volunteer opportunities on a site like
VolunteerMatch or Idealist, or directly contact

00:05:41.849 --> 00:05:42.849
organizations that serve immigrants who speak
the language you want to learn.

00:05:42.849 --> 00:05:43.849
Visit businesses where people speak primarily
your target language.

00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:44.849
Perhaps there’s a Mexican restaurant nearby
where you can enjoy delicious food and practice

00:05:44.849 --> 00:05:45.849
your Spanish with the waiters or owners, or
perhaps you can practice your Chinese at a

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:46.849
grocery store that sells food to the local
Chinese community.

00:05:46.849 --> 00:05:47.849
6.

00:05:47.849 --> 00:05:48.849
Learn about the culture.

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:49.849
Understanding a language is about more than
understanding words on a page.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:50.849
It’s important to learn about the culture
and history associated with these words.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:51.849
Knowing something about a country or culture’s
history, current events, religious beliefs

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:52.849
and common customs can help you understand
a lot about what people say and do.

00:05:52.849 --> 00:05:53.849
Researchers have found that children learn
to read in a second language better when they

00:05:53.849 --> 00:05:54.849
understand the culture and context behind
the pieces they read.

00:05:54.849 --> 00:05:55.849
As you begin to study a new language, take
some time to learn about the culture of the

00:05:55.849 --> 00:05:56.849
people who speak that language.

00:05:56.849 --> 00:05:57.849
Don’t feel this is a waste of time, even
if it involves reading and watching videos

00:05:57.849 --> 00:05:58.849
in your native language.

00:05:58.849 --> 00:05:59.849
It will help you enormously and can even prevent
you from making embarrassing and potentially

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:00.849
offensive mistakes.

00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:01.849
7.

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:02.849
Test yourself.

00:06:02.849 --> 00:06:03.849
Knowing that you plan to take a test is a
great way to motivate yourself to learn faster.

00:06:03.849 --> 00:06:04.849
Try to regularly test yourself in little ways.

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:05.849
If you’re learning from a textbook, take
practice tests or complete the exercises at

00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:06.849
the end of each chapter.

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:07.849
You can also play online games or take online
tests.

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:08.849
Online practice tests can be found in almost
any language, including French, Spanish, Japanese

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:09.849
and German.

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:10.849
Planning to take a standardized test several
months to a year after you begin learning

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:11.849
a new language can also keep you motivated,
and having the results can help you “prove”

00:06:11.849 --> 00:06:12.849
your language level to potential employers,
schools or even just yourself.

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:13.849
The ACTFL OPI test is popular in many language-learning
circles and widely respected.

00:06:13.849 --> 00:06:14.849
It tests oral proficiency and provides a score
that ranks your level anywhere from “Novice

00:06:14.849 --> 00:06:15.849
Low” to “Superior.”

00:06:15.849 --> 00:06:16.849
Some languages also have a standardized test
specific to that language, such as the JLPT

00:06:16.849 --> 00:06:17.849
for Japanese or the HSK for Chinese.

00:06:17.849 --> 00:06:18.849
Ask teachers or professionals who know the
language what tests they recommend.

00:06:18.849 --> 00:06:19.849
8.

00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:20.849
Have fun!

00:06:20.849 --> 00:06:21.849
We tend to learn best when we’re enjoying
ourselves, so don’t forget to make language

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:22.849
learning fun.

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:23.849
Playing games is a great way to have fun while
learning.

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:24.849
Games take advantage of our natural competitiveness
and can help us practice language skills even

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:25.849
when we feel tired.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:26.849
You can also focus your learning on things
that you find interesting, like a favorite

00:06:26.849 --> 00:06:27.849
hobby.

00:06:27.849 --> 00:06:28.849
If you like to sew, for example, study words
in your target language related to sewing,

00:06:28.849 --> 00:06:29.849
watch instructional sewing videos and talk
with tailors who speak your target language.

00:06:29.849 --> 00:06:30.849
If you’re learning French and fascinated
by French politics, learn words used to describe

00:06:30.849 --> 00:06:31.849
political processes, and immerse yourself
in articles about political issues, videos

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:32.849
of political debates and talk shows about
current events.

00:06:32.849 --> 00:06:33.849
Finally, make friends who speak your target
language or are interested in learning it.

00:06:33.849 --> 00:06:34.849
Languages aren’t meant to be learned in
a vacuum!

00:06:34.849 --> 00:06:35.849
Real-life social events and conversations
are what make language learning fun and worthwhile.

00:06:35.849 --> 00:06:36.849
Make a point of talking to people and learning
more about their lives and cultures.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:37.849
You might be surprised at how excited they
are to share information with you, and how

00:06:37.849 --> 00:06:37.857
quickly you make lasting friendships in the
process.

